Certainly. Below is a concise Java code snippet that leverages Java Streams to check if a list contains not more than one entry. If the list contains more than one entry, an exception is thrown. If exactly one entry is present, it is returned; if the list is empty, null is returned.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class SingleElementChecker {
    public static <T> T getSingleOrNull(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                .limit(2)
                .reduce((a, b) -> {
                    throw new IllegalArgumentException("List contains more than one element");
                })
                .orElse(null);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> emptyList = List.of();
        List<String> singleList = List.of("one");
        List<String> multipleList = List.of("one", "two");

        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(emptyList)); // prints: null
        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(singleList)); // prints: one
        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(multipleList)); // throws IllegalArgumentException
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- `limit(2)` ensures the stream processes at most two elements, for performance.
- `reduce((a, b) -> { throw new IllegalArgumentException(...); })` throws an exception if a second element is found.
- `.orElse(null)` returns the value if present, otherwise null for empty lists.

This approach is efficient and idiomatic for Java Streams.

